  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>A <strong>cavernoma</strong> (cavernous malformation) is a cluster of dilated vessels with very thin walls. It looks like a raspberry or mulberry, from a few millimetres to a few centimetres in size.</p>
    <p>Blood flows slowly in a cavernoma. The thin walls sometimes "leak" — small bleeds happen in and around it. They are usually limited, but if the cavernoma lies in an important area — for example the brainstem — even a small bleed can cause noticeable symptoms.</p>
    <p>Cavernomas can be single or multiple. Multiple ones are often linked to a <strong>familial form</strong>, which is inherited.</p>
    <p>A <strong>developmental venous anomaly</strong> — a variation in the structure of a vein — is often found next to a cavernoma. It is harmless, and normal drainage of the brain sometimes goes through it, so it is preserved during surgery.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>Many cavernomas cause no symptoms and are found by chance on MRI. If there are symptoms, most often these are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>epileptic seizures</strong> — especially with cavernomas in the cerebral cortex;</li>
      <li><strong>a bleed</strong> — a sudden headache, weakness or numbness, problems with speech, vision or coordination;</li>
      <li><strong>gradually increasing neurological problems</strong> — with repeated small bleeds, especially in the brainstem: double vision, facial asymmetry, swallowing problems, unsteadiness.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI</strong> — the main test. A cavernoma looks typical: a mottled lesion with a dark rim of iron deposits from old bleeds.</li>
      <li><strong>SWI or SWAN sequences</strong> — special MRI sequences sensitive to blood breakdown products. They find even small cavernomas not visible on standard sequences. Worth doing if multiple cavernomas are suspected.</li>
      <li><strong>CT</strong> — if a fresh bleed is suspected.</li>
      <li><strong>Angiography</strong> does not show a cavernoma, but is sometimes done to rule out other vascular causes.</li>
      <li><strong>EEG</strong> — if there were seizures.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An MRI report is not yet a diagnosis. The diagnosis is made by a doctor based on images and symptoms together.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <h3>Observation</h3>
    <p>If a cavernoma causes no symptoms, it is usually observed: repeat MRI on the schedule set by the doctor and a visit if new complaints appear. If there were seizures, a neurologist chooses antiepileptic treatment.</p>
    <h3>Surgery</h3>
    <p>Removal is usually discussed if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>it causes epileptic seizures, especially ones poorly controlled by medication;</li>
      <li>there has been a bleed with neurological problems, especially a repeated one;</li>
      <li>the cavernoma is growing and lies where it can be removed with acceptable risk.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The operation is microsurgical, with neuronavigation: the cavernoma is removed entirely along with the altered tissue around it. Cavernomas in the brainstem and deep areas are removed only on strict indications — the cost of error there is especially high.</p>
    <h3>Radiosurgery</h3>
    <p>Precise irradiation is sometimes used for cavernomas that cannot be removed safely. Its effectiveness for cavernomas is lower than for AVMs, so it is chosen rarely and individually.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Follow-up MRI — to confirm complete removal.</li>
      <li>If there were seizures, antiepileptic treatment continues; the decision to stop it is made by a neurologist.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>A sudden severe headache, weakness or numbness of an arm, leg or half of the face, speech problems, double vision, first-ever seizures, loss of consciousness — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>
